.TH MAKE 1
.SH NAME
make \- maintain groups of programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B make
[\ \-f\ makefile\ ] [\ \-eiknpqrst\ ] [\ name=value\ ...\ ] [\ target\ ...\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B make
keeps the files of a program up to date with the files they are made
from. It reads a description of how each file, a
.IR target ,
is made from others, its
.IR prerequisites ,
and remakes the targets that are older than any of their prerequisites,
by running the commands the description gives for them. Prerequisites
are brought up to date first, in the same way, so that only what has
changed, and what depends on it, is made again.
.PP
make makes each
.I target
named on the command line, or, with none named, the first target in
the description. The description is read from the file
.BR makefile ,
or from
.B Makefile
if there is no
.BR makefile ,
unless
.B \-f
names another. Arguments of the form
.I name=value
define macros, as described below.
.PP
The options are:
.TP 12
.BI \-f " makefile"
Read the description from
.IR makefile .
A
.I makefile
of \- is the standard input. More than one
.B \-f
may be given; the files are read in order, as one description.
.TP
.B \-e
Let the environment's variables override the macros defined in the
description.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore the exit status of commands: go on as if each had succeeded, as
if every command began with \-.
.TP
.B \-k
When a command fails, abandon only the targets that depend on it, and
go on making the others.
.TP
.B \-n
Print the commands that would be run, without running them. Lines
beginning with @ are printed too.
.TP
.B \-p
Print the complete set of macros and rules, those built in included,
before making anything.
.TP
.B \-q
Question: run nothing, and exit with status 0 if the targets are up to
date, 1 if they are not.
.TP
.B \-r
Do not use the built-in suffix rules and macros.
.TP
.B \-s
Silent: do not print the commands before running them, as if every
command began with @.
.TP
.B \-t
Touch the targets: change their times to now instead of running their
commands, so that they are taken as up to date.
.SS Rules
A rule is a line of target names, a colon, and the names of the
prerequisites, followed by the commands that make the targets, each on
a line beginning with a tab:
.PP
.nf
	prog: main.o util.o
		cc \-o prog main.o util.o
	main.o util.o: defs.h
.fi
.PP
A target is remade when it does not exist, or when any prerequisite is
newer than it. A target may appear in several rules with a single colon;
their prerequisites are added together, and only one of the rules may
have commands. With two colons,
.BR :: ,
each rule stands alone: its commands are run when the target is older
than one of that rule's own prerequisites.
.PP
Each command line is printed, then run. A command containing none of
the shell's special characters is run directly; any other is given to
.B "/bin/sh \-c"
to run. Each line is a separate command: a
.B cd
on one line does not affect the next. A command line beginning with
.B @
is not printed; one beginning with
.B \-
may fail without stopping make. Otherwise, when a command exits with a
status other than 0, make stops, unless
.B \-i
or
.B \-k
was given.
.PP
A line ending with \e is joined to the next. # begins a comment, to the
end of the line. A line
.PP
.nf
	include \fIfile\fP
.fi
.PP
reads the description in
.I file
in its place.
.SS Macros
A line
.PP
.nf
	\fIname\fP = \fIvalue\fP
.fi
.PP
defines a macro. Elsewhere,
.BI $( name )
or
.BI ${ name }
is replaced by the macro's value, and
.BI $ x
for a macro whose name is the single character
.IR x .
.BI $( name : old = new )
is the value with the ending
.I old
of each word changed to
.IR new ,
as in $(OBJS:.o=.c). A macro that is not defined is empty. $$ is a $.
.PP
Macros are taken from the built-in rules, then the environment, then
the description, then the command line, each overriding those before
it; with
.BR \-e ,
the environment overrides the description.
.PP
In the commands of a rule, make defines these:
.TP 8
.B $@
the name of the target
.TP
.B $*
the target's name without its suffix (in a suffix rule)
.TP
.B $<
the prerequisite that made the target out of date; in a suffix rule,
the source file
.TP
.B $?
the prerequisites that are newer than the target
.TP
.B $(@D)
the directory part of $@, or . if it has none
.TP
.B $(@F)
the file name part of $@
.SS Suffix rules
A target that has no commands of its own is made by a suffix rule, if
one fits. The rule
.B .c.o:
tells how to make a file whose name ends in .o from one, with the same
name before the suffix, ending in .c; the rule
.B .c:
how to make a file with no suffix from one ending in .c. The suffixes
that count, in the order they are tried, are the prerequisites of the
special target
.BR .SUFFIXES ;
a
.B .SUFFIXES
rule adds to the list, and one with no prerequisites empties it. The
built-in macros and rules are:
.PP
.nf
	.SUFFIXES: .o .c .s
	MAKE = make
	CC = cc
	CFLAGS = \-O
	AS = as86
	LD = ld86
	AR = ar86
	ARFLAGS = rv
	.c.o:
		$(CC) $(CFLAGS) \-c $<
	.s.o:
		$(CC) \-c $<
	.c:
		$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $(LDFLAGS) \-o $@ $<
	.s:
		$(CC) $(LDFLAGS) \-o $@ $<
.fi
.SS Special targets
.TP 12
.B .DEFAULT
Its commands make any target that has no rule and no file.
.TP
.B .IGNORE
Ignore the exit status of every command, as with
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B .SILENT
Print no commands, as with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B .PRECIOUS
When make is interrupted while making a target, it removes the target,
which may be half made, unless the target is a prerequisite of
.BR .PRECIOUS .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every environment variable is a macro, as described above.
.SH FILES
.TP 12
makefile
.PD 0
.TP
Makefile
the description, if \-f is not given
.PD
.SH EXAMPLES
A library and a program that uses it:
.PP
.nf
	OBJS = main.o parse.o
	LIB = libutil.a
	LOBJS = strsave.o error.o

	prog: $(OBJS) $(LIB)
		$(CC) \-o prog $(OBJS) $(LIB)

	$(LIB): $(LOBJS)
		$(AR) $(ARFLAGS) $(LIB) $?

	$(OBJS): defs.h

	clean:
		rm \-f prog $(OBJS) $(LOBJS) $(LIB)
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
make exits with status 0 when all the targets were made, 1 under
.B \-q
when one is out of date, and 2 after an error. Its messages include:
.TP 8
.B "Don't know how to make \fItarget\fP.  Stop."
There is no file named
.IR target ,
no rule for it, and no suffix rule that fits.
.TP
.B "\fItarget\fP depends on itself"
The rules form a loop.
.TP
.B "\fItarget\fP not remade because of errors"
Under
.BR \-k ,
a prerequisite of the target failed.
.TP
.B "makefile, line \fIn\fP: \fImessage\fP"
The description has a line make does not understand.
.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), cc(1), ar86(1), touch(1)
.PP
S. I. Feldman,
.IR "Make \(em A Program for Maintaining Computer Programs" ,
Bell Laboratories.
.SH HISTORY
A
.B make
command first appeared outside of Bell Laboratories in PWB/UNIX 1.0. It
was written by Stuart Feldman, in 1976.
.SH AUTHORS
This make was written for the xenix86-devsys Development System,
following the make of Version 7 AT&T UNIX and System V.
.SH BUGS
make decides what is out of date by the files' times, so the system
clock must be right. A file whose time is in the future always looks up
to date.
.PP
A make run by a command in a description does not inherit the options
of the one that ran it.
.PP
File names with spaces in them cannot be targets or prerequisites.
